sections.body:
Feature flags (also called feature toggles) are the mechanism that makes trunk-based development practical even for large or risky features: merge the code continuously, even while it's incomplete, but gate its execution behind a runtime condition so it has zero user-visible effect until someone deliberately turns it on.

```python
def get_checkout_page(user):
    if feature_flags.is_enabled("new_checkout_flow", user):
        return render_new_checkout(user)
    return render_legacy_checkout(user)
```

This single idea separates two questions that used to be conflated: 'is this code merged and deployed to production' and 'is this feature actually live for users' -- with a feature flag, the answer to the first can be yes for weeks while the answer to the second stays no, with no long-lived branch required at all. This is also the mechanism that connects directly to canary-style rollout, covered below: a flag can be enabled for 1% of users, then 10%, then everyone, all without any additional deploy. One real cost worth naming: flags accumulate. Once a feature is fully rolled out and stable, the flag and the old code path it guards need to actually be deleted -- teams that skip this step end up with a codebase full of permanent branching logic for features that have been 'done' for a year, sometimes called flag debt.

